{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/SzeChunYiu/ORION/pulls/1466","id":4371011358,"node_id":"PR_kwDOT5fCLM8AAAABBIhXHg","html_url":"https://github.com/SzeChunYiu/ORION/pull/1466","diff_url":"https://github.com/SzeChunYiu/ORION/pull/1466.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/SzeChunYiu/ORION/pull/1466.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/SzeChunYiu/ORION/issues/1466","number":1466,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"[R12][D] Exact first-mixing certificates for typed authority bridges","user":{"login":"SzeChunYiu","id":52784502,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNzg0NTAy","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52784502?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/SzeChunYiu","html_url":"https://github.com/SzeChunYiu","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SzeChunYiu/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SzeChunYiu/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SzeChunYiu/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SzeChunYiu/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SzeChunYiu/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SzeChunYiu/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SzeChunYiu/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SzeChunYiu/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SzeChunYiu/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Clean child of #1452 at exact parent `f6b21c94b9cd372700d7a13ccc229e27637acef9`.\n\nR11 proved the pinned `agentgateway` native authorization merge is origin-witness safe. R12 sharpens Paper D to the remaining cross-field question: when pooled evidence authorizes but no origin independently does, where does the first unauthorized combination occur?\n\n## Exact theorem increment\n\nFor a finite acyclic positive Horn program and independent seed origins, define\n\n`W(a) = {origin lambda : lambda's independent closure derives a}`.\n\nR12 proves:\n\n1. exact topological recurrence: each rule contributes the intersection of its premise witness sets;\n2. an atom is hybrid iff it is pooled-derivable and `W(a)` is empty;\n3. every hybrid authorization has a **first-mixing ancestor** whose premises each have nonempty witnesses but whose witness intersection is empty;\n4. an alternative complete one-origin proof prevents a hybrid verdict, even if a mixed proof also exists;\n5. all witnesses and a local certificate are computed in bitset-linear time for one fixed acyclic program;\n6. origin-witness-preserving modules compose through overlap-safe bridges;\n7. a hybrid output localizes a failed upstream, bridge-overlap or downstream preservation obligation.\n\nThis audits one proposed pooled state. It does not replace the R10 NP-completeness result for finding a minimum dangerous origin subset.\n\n## Source-bound real bridge control\n\nAt pinned `agentgateway/agentgateway@e136c7458b0fe0f51378dd31ffd60ab2b6939fc2`:\n\n- `Jwt::apply` validates one token into one `Claims` value and inserts it as a typed request extension;\n- the CEL executor exposes one typed `jwt: ExtensionOrDirect<jwt::Claims>` field;\n- HTTP authorization evaluates against one request executor;\n- native authorization rule sets retain the R11 safe merge semantics.\n\nTherefore this exact bridge is not a coordinatewise union of arbitrary JWT claim maps. If token identity is the claim origin, JWT conjunctions read one active singleton-origin slot and cannot be cross-token first-mixing rules. This is a SAFE source-bound bridge result, not a whole-gateway security certification.\n\n## Exact finite result\n\nWorkflow run `33017457616` is GREEN and now requires byte-exact equality between two fresh regenerations and the committed result.\n\n- source-bound predecessor run/job: `33017186963` / `98338314999`;\n- systems: `10,192`;\n- hybrid atoms: `68`;\n- first-mixing certificates: `68/68`;\n- unary origin-preserving controls: `2,000`;\n- unary-control hybrids: `0`;\n- result SHA-256: `df15ba9dce6fa471087819cd7b7f517d5125eef93adb8cfa150155dcca27e291`;\n- source-bound artifact SHA-256: `2abdefc62c248d1f528dcdbe8f69b90c425afda6fe2f10fc75511f5cc8271d58`.\n\nTerminal:\n\n`TYPED_AUTHORITY_FIRST_MIXING_R12_PASS`\n\n## Hostile/null controls\n\n- token A supplies subject, token B supplies admin scope: coordinate erasure creates a first-mixing authorization;\n- either single token slot alone denies;\n- adding token C with both claims makes authorization nonhybrid, despite the still-existing A+B mixed proof;\n- an explicit bridge-license origin carrying the complete premise tuple is not mislabeled splicing;\n- the R11 native authorization merge remains a real SAFE null control.\n\n## Prior-art and authority boundary\n\nGeneric Horn closure, why/where provenance, provenance semirings, Datalog witness explanations, truth maintenance and bitset data-flow are donor-owned. R12 does not claim generic provenance propagation or first-cause tracing as new.\n\nThe residual candidate contribution is the singleton-origin authorization predicate, its distinction from merely mixed proofs, the first-mixing certificate tied to typed merge safety, and the source-bound SAFE/unsafe bridge discriminator.\n\nNo deployed vulnerability, whole-system security certification, external domain adjudication, novelty certificate, production authority or journal authority is claimed.\n\n## Remaining top-tier systems gate\n\nThe next application must use an independently maintained integration where multiple real evidence records reach one decision under both origin-preserving and coordinate-erased implementations, with cases and adjudication frozen before outcomes. A further same-owner synthetic corpus cannot close this gate.\n\nPart of #1387 and #1421.","created_at":"2026-08-26T21:50:49Z","updated_at":"2026-08-26T21:59:04Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":"8bb5af5da68d77cec2f26547a386eaad91dfcd4c","assignees":[]